user:
---
The target function is `cJSON_Compare`. `cJSON_Compare` checks whether two cJSON values are equal. Use it with valid `cJSON*` nodes; it returns false if either is NULL, types differ, or type is invalid. If `a==b`, it returns true. For objects, `case_sensitive` controls key matching. Its signature is as follows:

```c
/* Duplicate will create a new, identical cJSON item to the one you pass, in new memory that will
 * need to be released. With recurse!=0, it will duplicate any children connected to the item.
 * The item->next and ->prev pointers are always zero on return from Duplicate. */
/* Recursively compare two cJSON items for equality. If either a or b is NULL or invalid, they will be considered unequal.
 * case_sensitive determines if object keys are treated case sensitive (1) or case insensitive (0) */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_Compare(const cJSON * const a, const cJSON * const b, const cJSON_bool case_sensitive);
```

The list of candidate functions from the library is:

1. `cJSON_GetArrayItem`
    `cJSON_GetArrayItem` fetches the array element at a 0-based index. Use it with a `cJSON` array and nonnegative `index`. If `index < 0` or the item doesn’t exist, it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Retrieve item number "index" from array "array". Returns NULL if unsuccessful. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_GetArrayItem(const cJSON *array, int index);
    ```
2. `cJSON_Parse`
    `cJSON_Parse` is a convenience wrapper for `cJSON_ParseWithOpts(value, 0, 0)`. Use it to parse a JSON string into a `cJSON*` tree. On success, free with `cJSON_Delete`; on invalid JSON, it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Memory Management: the caller is always responsible to free the results from all variants of cJSON_Parse (with cJSON_Delete) and cJSON_Print (with stdlib free, cJSON_Hooks.free_fn, or cJSON_free as appropriate). The exception is cJSON_PrintPreallocated, where the caller has full responsibility of the buffer. */
/* Supply a block of JSON, and this returns a cJSON object you can interrogate. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_Parse(const char *value);
    ```
3. `cJSON_DetachItemFromArray`
    Detaches and returns the `which`th item from a cJSON array without deleting it. Use with a valid array and nonnegative index. If `which < 0`, it returns `NULL`; if the index is out of range, the underlying lookup yields `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_DetachItemFromArray(cJSON *array, int which);
    ```
4. `cJSON_CreateObjectReference`
    Creates a reference wrapper for an existing cJSON object/array. Use it when you want `cJSON_Delete` to free only the wrapper, not the referenced children. If allocation fails, it returns `NULL`; it sets type to `cJSON_Object | cJSON_IsReference` and stores `child`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Create an object/array that only references it's elements so
 * they will not be freed by cJSON_Delete */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateObjectReference(const cJSON *child);
    ```
5. `cJSON_CreateNull`
    `cJSON_CreateNull()` creates a new cJSON item of type `cJSON_NULL`. Use it when you need to build a JSON null value in a cJSON tree. It returns the new item on success, or `NULL` if allocation fails. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* These calls create a cJSON item of the appropriate type. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateNull(void);
    ```
6. `cJSON_IsArray`
    `cJSON_IsArray` checks whether a `cJSON*` is a non-NULL array node. Use it before treating an item as an array. It returns `false` for `NULL` or any non-array type, `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_Array`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsArray(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
7. `cJSON_CreateArrayReference`
    Creates a cJSON array node that **references** an existing child list, not a copy. Use it when you want an array wrapper around already-built items. If allocation fails, returns `NULL`. The referenced child is marked as `cJSON_IsReference`, so it won’t be freed as owned data. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateArrayReference(const cJSON *child);
    ```
8. `cJSON_AddFalseToObject`
    `cJSON_AddFalseToObject` creates a JSON `false` item and adds it to an object under `name`. Use it to set a boolean false member. On success it returns the new item; on failure it returns `NULL` and frees the item. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddFalseToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name);
    ```
9. `cJSON_InitHooks`
    Sets cJSON’s global memory hooks. Call with `NULL` to reset to `malloc/free/realloc`. Otherwise provide custom `malloc_fn`/`free_fn`; `realloc` is enabled only when both are the defaults, else disabled (`NULL`). Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Supply malloc, realloc and free functions to cJSON */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_InitHooks(cJSON_Hooks* hooks);
    ```
10. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject`
    Adds a reference to `item` into `object` under `string`. Use it when you want the same JSON value shared, not copied. Returns false if `object` or `string` is NULL; otherwise it wraps `item` via `create_reference` and adds it. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
    ```
11. `cJSON_GetStringValue`
    `cJSON_GetStringValue` returns `item->valuestring` only if `item` is a JSON string; otherwise it returns `NULL`. Use it to safely read a string value after checking type (or let it do the check). It does not copy or modify the string. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Check item type and return its value */
CJSON_PUBLIC(char *) cJSON_GetStringValue(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
12. `cJSON_AddBoolToObject`
    Adds a JSON boolean field to an object. Use with a valid object and key name; it creates a bool item from `boolean`, inserts it, and returns the new item. On insert failure, it deletes the item and returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddBoolToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name, const cJSON_bool boolean);
    ```
13. `cJSON_PrintBuffered`
    `cJSON_PrintBuffered` renders a cJSON tree to text with a preallocated buffer. Use it when you have a rough size estimate: larger `prebuffer` can reduce reallocations. `fmt=0` gives compact output, `fmt=1` pretty-prints. Returns `NULL` on negative `prebuffer`, alloc failure, or print failure. Its signature is:
    ```c
