Task: Write a C fuzz driver program to fuzz the library cjson using the information provided below. Reply with only the content of the C source file enclosed in triple backticks (```), without any additional explanation.

## Target API Functions

You should invoke the functions in the following order: `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_Minify` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete`

Below is the detailed information:

1. `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS`
​ Adds `item` to `object` using a const key string. Use when the key is a literal or otherwise guaranteed to outlive the JSON object. It sets the string as const, so cJSON won’t free it; don’t write to `item->string` unless `cJSON_StringIsConst` is clear.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Use this when string is definitely const (i.e. a literal, or as good as), and will definitely survive the cJSON object.
 * WARNING: When this function was used, make sure to always check that (item->type & cJSON_StringIsConst) is zero before
 * writing to `item->string` */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
2. `cJSON_AddItemToObject`
​ Adds `item` to a JSON object under key `string`. Use it to build objects after creating both the object and item. It returns a `cJSON_bool` success flag; the item is attached to `object` via the given key.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
3. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject`
​ Deletes the first object member with the given key. Use on a cJSON object and key string; it detaches that item from the object, then frees it with `cJSON_Delete`. If the key isn’t found, nothing happens.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject(cJSON *object, const char *string);
​ ```
4. `cJSON_Minify`
​ `cJSON_Minify` edits a writable JSON buffer in place to remove whitespace and C-style comments, compacting the text. Use it only on mutable memory; `NULL` is ignored. It skips spaces/newlines and `//` or `/* */` comments, preserving other characters.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Minify a strings, remove blank characters(such as ' ', '\t', '\r', '\n') from strings.
 * The input pointer json cannot point to a read-only address area, such as a string constant,
 * but should point to a readable and writable address area. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_Minify(char *json);
​ ```
5. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray`
​ `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray(array, which)` removes the item at index `which` from a JSON array and frees it. It’s a convenience wrapper around `cJSON_DetachItemFromArray()` + `cJSON_Delete()`. If the index is invalid, nothing is deleted.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray(cJSON *array, int which);
​ ```
6. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray`
​ Adds a reference wrapper of `item` to `array`, so the original cJSON isn’t moved or corrupted. Use it when reusing an existing item in another array. Returns false if `array` is NULL; otherwise it appends a reference created from `item`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Append reference to item to the specified array/object. Use this when you want to add an existing cJSON to a new cJSON, but don't want to corrupt your existing cJSON. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray(cJSON *array, cJSON *item);
​ ```
7. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject`
​ Adds a reference to `item` into `object` under `string`. Use it when you want the same JSON value shared, not copied. Returns false if `object` or `string` is NULL; otherwise it wraps `item` via `create_reference` and adds it.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
8. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive`
​ Deletes a case-sensitive object member by key. Use with a JSON object and key string; it detaches the matching item from the object, then frees it via `cJSON_Delete`. If no matching key exists, nothing is deleted.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive(cJSON *object, const char *string);
​ ```
9. `cJSON_Delete`
​ `cJSON_Delete` frees a cJSON item and its linked subitems recursively. Use it on a root node when done. It skips freeing `child/valuestring/string` for `cJSON_IsReference` or `cJSON_StringIsConst` items, then frees the node itself.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Delete a cJSON entity and all subentities. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_Delete(cJSON *item);
​ ```

## Related Data Types

```c
/* The cJSON structure: */
struct cJSON {
 /* next/prev allow you to walk array/object chains. Alternatively, use GetArraySize/GetArrayItem/GetObjectItem */
 struct cJSON *next;
